pouch boundary-line patch 0064 - posix_spawn sends envp as the child's whole environment (env-c, operator vote 28).

0026 dropped envp: a child's environment was the kernel's rfork copy of the
spawner's /env, so a caller that passed a different environment got its own,
and a setenv never reached a child (seam-pouch-spawn-envp). The kernel's
SYS_SPAWN_FULL_ARGV now takes a SPAWN_EXT_ENV tail (ext_flags bit 2,
dec-2026-10-28-spawn-env) whose NUL-packed NAME=VALUE records are the child's
whole /env. Two files:

  src/internal/_pouch_process.h  POUCH_SPAWN_EXT_ENV, POUCH_SPAWN_ENV_DATA_MAX,
      struct pouch_spawn_ext_env (the kernel's sys_spawn_ext_env, every field
      pinned; tools/check-spawn-args-mirrors.py holds it to the kernel), and
      struct pouch_spawn_env_rec, the record with the tail directly after it.
  src/process/posix_spawn.c      packs envp the way it packs argv and always
      sends the tail: a later record for a name replaces an earlier one, and a
      NULL envp is an empty environment, as Linux's execve reads it. A block
      past 32 KiB is E2BIG here; the kernel's refusals of the tail (a record
      with no '=', a name /env refuses, a value past 4096, a 65th name) come
      back as their errno instead of the record's flat ENOENT.

The witness is pouch-hello-spawn's test 3: a block with a repeated name, the
caller's environ after a setenv, a NULL envp, and four refusals that make no
child, against a variable that is in the parent's /env but not its environ.

